Statements:
[
    "LLM compare is a new method for estimating problem difficulty that does not rely on ground truth data, such as performance scores or reference answers.",
    "In this method, an LLM is repeatedly asked to compare two problems and then difficulty scores are computed based on the outcomes, using the Bradley–Terry (BT) model.",
    "The method consists of two distinct parts: Pairwise Comparisons and Bradley-Terry Fitting.",
    "An LLM performs pairwise difficulty comparisons between problems.",
    "Bradley–Terry strengths are fitted to the outcomes of these comparisons.",
    "The resulting BT scores serve as the final difficulty scores for the problems.",
    "The higher the BT score, the higher the inferred difficulty.",
    "The differences in difficulty between problems are interpretable as the BT scores are linear in the log-odds of success.",
    "LLM compare naturally occupies two empty quadrants in the space of difficulty measures.",
    "It is the first measure that is Dynamic, Continuous, Model-agnostic, and Independent of ground truth.",
    "With each new question scored, the BT scores recalibrate.",
    "Output is on a continuous scale.",
    "Does not depend on a specific model.",
    "Uses a basic prompt without requiring reference answers.",
    "This makes it the only measure currently suitable for scoring synthetic out-of-distribution problems.",
    "Of all the LLM-based difficulty measures, LLM compare is the most robust to hallucinations.",
    "This robustness is primarily because it is a more fine-grained measure.",
    "When noise (LLM hallucinations) is introduced, LLM performance results in a wrong answer being made correct or vice versa.",
    "When noise is introduced, LLM labels result in a wrong label being assigned to a question.",
    "When noise is introduced, LLM compare results in the wrong question being picked in the difficulty comparison.",
    "Testing was conducted by adding 1-10% hallucinations to each LLM-based measure, and LLM compare degraded the least relative to the original sequence.",
    "According to all three correlation metrics (Pearson, Spearman, and Kendall), the LLM compare difficulty scores degrade the least compared to the original sequence.",
    "LLM compare correlates positively with all types of existing difficulty measures and is the most robust to hallucinations out of all LLM-based measures.",
    "It achieves a Pearson correlation of over 80% with human labels.",
    "LLM compare is an important step towards solving the need for time-consuming human annotations as it shows high correlation to human labels and is robust to hallucinations.",
    "The BT scores reflect the presence of tiers in benchmark difficulty, and the relative differences in difficulty between problems are interpretable, in contrast to ranking- or binning-based difficulty measures."
] 

