Statements:
[
    "Learning reaction-diffusion (RD) systems from data requires ensuring physical consistency and well-posedness of the learned models.",
    "Standard parameterization approaches for reaction terms (such as neural networks) do not typically reflect these physical constraints.",
    "The global existence of solutions for RD systems requires sufficient regularity - specifically local Lipschitz continuity.",
    "The global existence of solutions for RD systems requires growth condition.",
    "The global existence of solutions for RD systems requires mass control condition - ensures total mass remains bounded, or is neither dissipated nor generated.",
    "The global existence of solutions for RD systems requires quasipositivity - guarantees that non-negativity of initial conditions is preserved for the solution.",
    "Conditions iii) and iv) have physical significance, while i) and ii) are purely technical in nature.",
    "The framework directly incorporates these conditions within the parameterized reaction fθ.",
    "Embedding these conditions into fθ is essential for maintaining physical consistency in the learned models.",
    "Embedding these conditions into fθ ensures that for some fixed fθ and D, the resulting RD system ∂tu = D∆u + fθ(u) is well-posed.",
    "The work proposes techniques to systematically modify a given class of parameterized reaction terms such that the resulting terms inherently satisfy mass conservation and quasipositivity.",
    "These modifications also guarantee well-posedness of the resulting PDEs under additional regularity and growth conditions.",
    "Modification techniques ensure that mass conservation, quasipositivity, sufficient regularity, and growth conditions are inherently embedded in the parameterized reaction terms fθ.",
    "This guarantees that the resulting RD systems are well-posed and physically consistent.",
    "Solutions to the learning problem converge to a unique, regularization-minimizing solution of a limit system even when conservation laws and quasipositivity are enforced.",
    "The framework provides approximation results for quasipositive functions, which are essential for constructing physically consistent parameterizations.",
    "This analysis-driven perspective distinguishes the work from existing approaches in conservation law guided model learning by addressing key physical constraints directly at the level of reaction term parameterization.",
    "The approach ensures physical consistency.",
    "The approach ensures well-posedness.",
    "The approach ensures interpretability of the resulting learned RD systems.",
    "The key innovation is incorporating physical properties such as mass conservation and quasipositivity directly into the parameterization of reaction terms, ensuring that learned models preserve non-negativity, adhere to physical principles, and remain well-posed under additional regularity and growth conditions."
] 

