Statements:
[
    "Maximum Mean Discrepancy (MMD) is the most commonly used metric for evaluating the generative process of Graph Generative Models (GGMs) in the literature.",
    "MMD is a distance-measure between distributions that captures the similarity between a specific property of the original and generated graphs.",
    "Low MMD values do not necessarily correlate with the model's ability to generate graphs that are structurally similar to the original ones.",
    "Both GRAN and EDGE achieve low MMD values across various topological metrics, suggesting good distributional alignment.",
    "Deeper analysis reveals significant limitations in their ability to preserve the essential structural properties that define different graph classes.",
    "This discrepancy highlights the limitations of MMD as a sole evaluation metric.",
    "Current techniques for evaluating GGMs often rely on MMD to assess the distribution of some graph properties in the generated ensemble, or even on visual inspection of the generated graphs.",
    "MMD fails to adequately assess the quality of generated graphs in terms of capturing the structural characteristics that distinguish different graph domains.",
    "MMD captures properties like degree distribution, clustering coefficient, and spectral properties.",
    "These metrics computed by comparing distributions don't ensure that individual generated graphs maintain the complex structural relationships present in real-world graphs.",
    "RGM (Representation-aware Graph-generation Model evaluation) is a novel approach for evaluating generated graphs by leveraging learned graph similarity.",
    "The core idea is to use a Siamese Network, a neural architecture that learns task-specific similarity by comparing the representation of input pairs, to compute a similarity score between generated and original graphs in the embedding space.",
    "This allows assessment of how closely their learned representations align.",
    "The generation process should be able to produce graphs that resemble the original ones in some meaningful way.",
    "RGM uses a Geometric Deep Learning model trained on a custom dataset of synthetic and real-world graphs, specifically designed for graph classification tasks.",
    "RGM employs a more sophisticated model architecture and training methodology.",
    "RGM provides a more thorough evaluation that captures complex structural properties.",
    "Current graph generative models struggle to produce graphs that effectively capture the structural characteristics that distinguish different graph domains.",
    "MMD fails to adequately assess the quality of generated graphs.",
    "These findings have important implications for the graph generation community, suggesting that researchers should move beyond simple statistical measures and adopt more nuanced evaluation frameworks that can capture the complex structural properties that make graphs from different domains distinguishable.",
    "The insights gained from this evaluation could inform the development of improved Graph Generative Models that explicitly optimize for preserving domain-specific structural characteristics rather than just statistical properties."
] 

