Statements:
[
    "FLAME is a family of extremely lightweight and capable Time Series Foundation Models that support both deterministic and probabilistic forecasting via generative probabilistic modeling, ensuring both efficiency and robustness.",
    "The model achieves this through two key innovations: Legendre Memory in the backbone and a Normalization Flow-based forecasting head.",
    "Legendre Memory is a kind of continuous online function approximation that utilizes orthogonal Legendre polynomials to compress continuous-time history into state vectors with fixed sizes.",
    "When modeling sequence data with recurrent networks, "memory" denotes the online compression capability of historical observations.",
    "FLAME adapts variants of Legendre Memory—translated Legendre (LegT) and scaled Legendre (LegS)—in the Encoding and Decoding phases.",
    "This architecture compresses varying local environmental information into time series tokens.",
    "This architecture supports adaptive memory utilization in long-range forecasting.",
    "This architecture effectively captures the inherent inductive bias within data.",
    "This architecture enables efficient long-range inferences.",
    "Both the Local-Perception Module and SSD-Decoder are driven by Legendre Memory and able to adaptively utilize the inductive bias within data.",
    "The Flow-based Head models intricate token-wise distributions over the forecasting horizon, considering the causality within tokens, with strong efficiency and accuracy.",
    "The Flow-based Head preserves both accuracy and efficiency by constructing lightweight coupling layers to simulate the reversible conditional transfer process from fixed prior Gaussian distributions to any complex real-data distribution.",
    "FLAME adopts a Normalization Flow-based forecasting head, which can model arbitrarily intricate distributions over the forecasting horizon in a generative manner.",
    "Even the largest version of FLAME (10M parameters) is far more lightweight than other time series foundation models that support both deterministic and probabilistic forecasting.",
    "The model family includes three parameter versions: 2M, 6M, and 10M.",
    "The extreme efficiency owes to the lightweight backbone and Flow-based Head.",
    "Previous probabilistic foundation models possess heavy backbones with hundreds-million to billion parameters, which are not very efficient in making generative probabilistic forecasts on large downstream datasets.",
    "No existing methods explored how to achieve outstanding probabilistic forecasting performance with lightweight architectures before FLAME.",
    "The FLAME family consistently outperforms other advanced time series foundation models on datasets from TSFM-Bench.",
    "FLAME achieves the most 1st counts while remaining the most lightweight.",
    "FLAME Large achieves the most 1st counts in zero-shot probabilistic forecasting tasks, excelling in both periodic scenarios (like ETT and Weather) and non-stationary scenarios (like Exchange and ILI).",
    "Compared with previous baselines MOIRAI Large and Chronos Large, FLAME Large achieves significant average CRPS reductions of 34.3% and 18.1%.",
    "FLAME demonstrates state-of-the-art zero-shot performance on both deterministic and probabilistic forecasting benchmarks, including TSFM-Bench and ProbTS, demonstrating a strong out-of-the-box tool of decision intelligence.",
