Statements:
[
    "Gaussian Processes become difficult to interpret unless the kernel function is explicitly constrained to be additive over the input dimensions.",
    "Without such constraints, GPs behave as black box models, making it hard for decision-makers to understand why certain solutions are preferred.",
    "This is a critical issue in many real-world applications.",
    "Decision Trees (DTs) are inherently interpretable, able to capture discontinuities, and well suited for categorical data.",
    "The proposed method uses a single decision tree with probabilistic leaves, which is preferred over a completely probabilistic tree to retain a high degree of interpretability.",
    "The resulting surrogate model is a single decision tree with Gaussian distributions as probabilistic preference value predictions in each leaf.",
    "This structure allows decision-makers to trace exactly how preferences are partitioned across the input space.",
    "GPs struggle with handling qualitative data, especially when a qualitative factor contains many categories.",
    "Discrete data introduces discontinuities in the utility function, as the function values will now only change at discrete points.",
    "The most commonly used Radial Basis Function kernel and Matérn 2.5 kernels encode inherent smoothness assumptions (infinitely differentiable and twice differentiable, respectively) and are therefore incapable of modeling these discontinuities.",
    "Even without discrete data, the utility function can exhibit discontinuities.",
    "The tree-based surrogate model demonstrates capability of handling both qualitative and quantitative data out-of-the-box.",
    "A case study on the existing mixed-type Sushi dataset shows not only the high degree of interpretability of the proposed tree-based surrogate model but also its capability of handling both qualitative and quantitative data.",
    "Current Preferential Bayesian Optimization methods rely on Gaussian Processes as surrogate models, which are computationally complex, limiting their real-world usability.",
    "The posterior in GP-based approaches is a computationally intractable skew GP.",
    "The decision tree-based model scales much better on dataset size, making PBO possible on large datasets.",
    "This is a significant practical advantage for real-world applications involving many preference comparisons.",
    "The results show that the tree-based model performs slightly worse on non-spiky optimization functions but outperforms GP-based models on spiky functions.",
    "This makes the approach particularly suitable for utility functions with discontinuities or sharp transitions.",
    "The key advantages of decision tree-based surrogate models are inherent interpretability without requiring special kernel constraints.",
    "The key advantages include native handling of categorical data and discontinuities.",
    "The key advantages include better scalability to large datasets.",
    "The key advantages include superior performance on spiky/discontinuous functions."
] 

