    "Hopfield networks can infer the full isomorphism class of a graph from a small random sample.",
    "Invariance can be built into neural architectures.",
    "Invariance can emerge implicitly when training on group-structured data.",
    "Even without explicit regularization, certain learning dynamics have a preference for particular solutions.",
    "For classification using logistic loss, gradient descent drives the parameter norm to infinity while the parameter direction converges to the max-margin classifier.",
    "Standard learning rules for Hopfield networks are implicitly biased towards learning invariant representations when trained on group data.",
    "Using gradient descent to minimize energy flow (MEF) has an implicit bias toward norm-efficient solutions.",
    "This underpins a polynomial sample complexity bound for learning isomorphism classes.",
    "Graph isomorphism classes can be represented within a three-dimensional invariant subspace.",
    "As the sample size grows, learned parameters concentrate near this invariant subspace.",
    "As the sample size grows, the parameters returned by the optimizer converge onto a distinct subspace, identified as the parameters invariant to the underlying group action of the data.",
    "Histograms of weights concentrate about two values for several graph classes (chain, bipartite, Johnson) learned with different numbers of samples.",
    "Weight heatmaps for networks trained on clique and Paley data show this convergence pattern clearly as sample sizes increase from 10 to 50 to 600.",
    "The implicit bias toward norm efficiency enables a polynomial sample complexity in the number of vertices v.",
    "This supports a conjecture for the case of cliques.",
    "Any dataset which can be strictly memorized can be at least nearly strictly memorized using only a polynomial number of samples.",
    "The specific sample complexity varies with graph connectivity.",
    "Paley graphs need N = Ω̃(v^(2+ε)) samples.",
    "Cliques require N = Ω̃(v^(1+ε)) samples.",
    "Although Hopfield networks can memorize all classes, the sample complexity appears to vary with graph connectivity.",
    "For any sparsity level m, parameters lying on the invariant subspace can memorize any graph.",
    "Any graph isomorphism class can be strictly memorized by a Hopfield network.",
    "A bias toward norm efficiency in learning drives the emergence of approximate invariance under group-structured data.",
    "Any isomorphism class of a graph can be stored in a Hopfield network.",
    "This prompts investigation into using Hopfield networks to check for graph isomorphisms—a fundamental problem in computer science.",
    "This led to the Hopfield Network Not Graph Isomorphic Check (HNNGIC) algorithm.",
    "The critical number of samples for generalization appears to coincide with when these networks solve the Hidden Clique Problem (HCP).",
    "These critical sample counts can also be used to differentiate between combinatorial classes of graphs."
] 

