Statements:
[
    "Prevailing LLM architectures impose a rigid and fixed contextual structure by assigning linear or constant positional indices.",
    "The current linear context organization, which assigns consecutive integer indices to tokens, overlooks the internal structure of tokens based on their relevance.",
    "This limitation leads to noticeable performance degradation in tasks involving long-distance dependencies, a problem analogous to the high extraneous load issue in Cognitive Load Theory (CLT).",
    "From a probabilistic standpoint, these position assignment strategies—essentially uniform distributions over fixed integer ranges—are the least informative organizations of context and therefore limit representational efficiency.",
    "Drawing on Cognitive Load Theory (CLT), the authors argue that uninformative positional structure increases extraneous cognitive load, consuming finite working memory capacity that should be allocated to deep reasoning and attention allocation.",
    "CLT studies suggest that humans can actively reduce extraneous load by reorganizing context, e.g., grouping related information into meaningful chunks or removing irrelevant details from instructions.",
    "Since working memory capacity is fixed, the capacity saved through such reorganization can be reallocated to deeper reasoning processes associated with the germane load, thereby improving problem-solving performance.",
    "REPO proposes an internal mechanism for LLMs to reduce extraneous load by re-organizing the positions of tokens.",
    "This process is formalized as learning non-constrained position values based on information relevance of tokens, instead of using the fixed linear positions in prior work.",
    "Unlike standard approaches, REPO utilizes a differentiable module, fϕ, to assign token positions that capture contextual dependencies, rather than relying on pre-defined integer ranges.",
    "REPO allocates more attention to the most critical 'needle' tokens compared to baseline methods, while directing less attention to the nearest 'query' tokens.",
    "This behavior breaks the typical locality bias, dynamically adjusting based on the context.",
    "REPO mitigates locality bias of traditional position assignment strategies by allocating more attention to distant but relevant 'needle' tokens, and learns adaptive position patterns in a more non-linear and denser continuous space.",
    "The positions assigned by REPO exist in a denser, more non-linear space, which is critical for enhancing its generalization to longer contexts, such as extending from 4K to 16K tokens.",
    "REPO learns position patterns that resemble a hybrid of previous position assignment strategies, such as assigning constant positions a ± ε or enforcing a monotonic position sequence, within a given context span.",
    "The positions assigned by REPO capture the intrinsic structure of the input context (e.g., segmentation of few-shot examples).",
    "Even when all test contexts are within the training context length (i.e., 4K tokens), REPO outperforms RoPE by 11.04 points.",
    "This suggests that the re-positioning mechanism in REPO effectively reduces extraneous cognitive load and improves robustness against noisy or distracting information in model inputs.",
    "The advantage of REPO becomes even more pronounced on long-context tasks.",
    "REPO already surpasses all baselines at a context length of 4K, and the performance gap further widens at 8K and 16K, which are lengths unseen during training.",
    "When extending the testing context length to 16K tokens using the YaRN method, REPO outperforms other baselines by at least 13.25 EM points on QA and Needle-in-the-haystack (NIAH) tasks and 5.48 points on LongBench.",
    "REPO improves performance on structured data tasks by at least 1.16 points over baselines.",
    "Since linearizing structured data (e.g., tables and graphs) into natural language can result in substantial loss of structural information, contextual re-positioning benefits such data types.",
    "By enabling models to actively re-position their input context, REPO opens a new direction for flexible context management driven by innovations in LLM architecture.",
    "The germane load (e.g., the attention mechanism) can better process the context information with context re-positioning."
] 

