Statements:
[
    "The replacement of sign-based gradient scaling with a momentum-based adaptive matrix in adversarial attacks offers significant improvements in both theoretical convergence guarantees and practical transferability.",
    "The traditional PGD (Projected Gradient Descent) attack is essentially a reformulation of the regular PGM (Projected Gradient Method) that uses only the current gradient to determine its step-size.",
    "Sign-based gradient methods may not converge, even for simple convex problems.",
    "These methods require strong assumptions such as smoothness to guarantee convergence.",
    "The fixed step-size approach of traditional methods like I-FGSM and MI-FGSM prevents on-the-fly adaptation.",
    "By replacing the sign function with an adaptive diagonal matrix that uses accumulated gradients, these convergence issues can be addressed.",
    "Momentum-based adaptive matrices can provide optimal convergence for general convex problems, overcoming the non-convergence issues of methods like MI-FGSM.",
    "By utilizing the accumulated gradients, the adaptive matrix creates a more stable optimization process.",
    "Unlike scalar adaptive methods that scale all gradient elements uniformly, this approach provides element-wise adaptivity.",
    "The momentum-based adaptive matrix serves as a general and effective technique to boost adversarial transferability across different neural network architectures.",
    "The adaptive approach maintains better imperceptibility of the crafted adversarial examples.",
    "The proposed approach unites the momentum concept (from methods like MI-FGSM) with adaptive scaling (inspired by AdaGrad), creating a synergistic method that overcomes limitations of both previous approaches.",
    "Implementation involves calculating an adaptive diagonal matrix based on accumulated gradients, rather than simply taking the sign of the current gradient.",
    "Implementation involves incorporating momentum to further stabilize update directions and escape poor local optima.",
    "Implementation involves using dynamic, element-wise step-sizes rather than fixed, uniform scaling.",
    "The resulting approach (AdaMI) demonstrates optimal convergence for convex problems while generating adversarial examples with enhanced transferability across different neural network architectures."
] 

