Statements:
[
    "Implicit bias in Hopfield network training leads to the emergence of invariance and enables efficient learning of graph isomorphism classes from limited samples through several interconnected mechanisms.",
    "Gradient descent on the Minimize Energy Flow (MEF) objective inherently favors norm-efficient solutions, which proves crucial for learning graph isomorphism classes.",
    "The bias toward norm efficiency directs the optimization process toward solutions that possess a specific mathematical structure, resembling a hard-margin support vector machine (HSVM) on an induced linear representation.",
    "The bias creates an implicit generalization capacity that allows the network to memorize entire graph isomorphism classes from only a small random sample.",
    "Weight distributions concentrate around specific values for various graph classes (chain, bipartite, Johnson) as the sample size increases.",
    "As sample size grows (from 10 to 50 to 600), parameters converge onto a distinct subspace that corresponds to the parameters invariant to the underlying group action of the data.",
    "Only a polynomial number of samples is required for both HSVM and MEF approaches to memorize new samples with high probability.",
    "The sample size needed to memorize an isomorphism class is tiny relative to the class size.",
    "The MEF approach consistently achieves higher test accuracy with fewer samples compared to alternative methods.",
    "Graph isomorphism classes can be represented within a compact three-dimensional invariant subspace.",
    "The number of iterations was capped at 1000 in experiments, suggesting that the theoretical exponential dependency is highly pessimistic in practice.",
    "Different learning rules exhibit varying degrees of implicit bias strength, with MEF demonstrating superior performance.",
    "The implicit bias toward norm efficiency in learning drives the emergence of approximate invariance when training on group-structured data, enabling efficient learning of entire graph isomorphism classes from limited samples."
] 

