Statements:
[
    "CADYT (Causal Discovery for Dynamic Timeseries) leverages Gaussian Process-based continuous-time modeling to improve causal structure discovery compared to discrete-time Dynamic Bayesian Network (DBN) approaches through several key innovations, especially benefiting irregular sampling conditions.",
    "CADYT's primary distinction from traditional methods is its grounding in Difference-based Causal Models (DBCM) rather than discrete-time Dynamic Bayesian Networks.",
    "CADYT uses an instantaneous gradient assumption that aligns with the natural ODE structure of dynamical systems, which is more appropriate than the instantaneous-effect assumption required by DBNs.",
    "By building on the dynamic structural causal model (DSCM) framework, CADYT directly addresses the continuous nature of real-world physical systems.",
    "The method builds on the GP-based framework developed by Ensinger et al. (2024) to model continuous-time dynamics, chosen specifically for its ability to perform exact inference even under irregular sampling.",
    "The approach uses integrators that allow better representation of continuous dynamics and evaluation at any test point, which is critical for irregular sampling.",
    "CADYT's GP-based approach avoids imposing restrictive parametric assumptions on the dynamics functions.",
    "The method builds on the principle that the true causal graph minimizes Kolmogorov complexity.",
    "Since Kolmogorov complexity is not computable, CADYT uses MDL as a practical surrogate, developing a score specifically adapted for time-series trajectories.",
    "The approach minimizes the MDL-based score through structure search to identify the underlying causal structure.",
    "Experiments demonstrated that CADYT finds graphs closer to ground truth (lower NSHD) compared to baseline methods for both regularly and irregularly sampled data.",
    "The continuous-time approach allows the model to adapt to arbitrary timelines, eliminating the need for regular sampling assumptions that plague most existing methods.",
    "Performance shows gradual improvement with higher-order integrators (Adams-Bashforth of order 1, 2, and 3), demonstrating the benefit of more sophisticated continuous-time modeling.",
    "By combining these elements, CADYT achieves more accurate causal discovery in dynamical systems while maintaining effectiveness even with the challenging case of irregularly sampled data."
] 

